    "result_text": "All 263 tests pass \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**, including the two new acceptance tests (`AcceptanceTest`) and the owner controller tests. (The stack trace visible earlier in the tail was just an expected exception logged during a passing test, not a failure.)\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented owner deletion as a soft-delete across the Spring Boot app:\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean column (`@Column(name = \"deleted\")`) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()`. It defaults to `false`, so newly created owners are not deleted.\n\n2. **OpenAPI spec** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added a `deleted` boolean property to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries the field and MapStruct maps it automatically. It surfaces in `GET /api/owners/{id}` responses.\n\n3. **Database schemas** \u2014 added a `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` column to the `owners` table in all four schema files (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres) since `ddl-auto` is `none` for the test profile.\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.deleteOwner`** \u2014 instead of hard-deleting via the service, it now sets `deleted = true` and saves the owner, retaining the row and returning `204 No Content` (2xx). `GET` afterward still returns the owner with `deleted: true`.\n\n5. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 the household-duplicate check and the identity-in-use check now filter out soft-deleted owners (`!existing.isDeleted()`), so a normally-blocking duplicate is permitted when the only matching owner has been deleted.\n\nNote the service-layer `ClinicService.deleteOwner` (a hard delete) is intentionally left unchanged \u2014 an existing service test (`shouldDeleteOwner`) relies on it actually removing the record; the soft-delete behavior lives at the REST endpoint per the spec.",
